HEAD2 = "After BISITE and the seven Dérivateurs returns (added 11 November 2026)"
STATUS2 = ("All seven packages of the companion work on *Les Dérivateurs* have been answered (GPT-6 Astra, ChatGPT Pro mode) and reviewed by "
 "Claude (Anthropic). The last, BISITE (the two fine localizations over a base; smooth and proper functors; 126 statements), was reviewed only in part. "
 "The sections named below are to be attached under `BISITE_return/` with the review (`BISITE_review/`); statements are cited by their numbers. "
 "Where a combination of these results is an argument of the reviewer, it is called a lead: it is unverified, and is to be proved or refuted. "
 "The asks that follow from this section are inside the Parts, marked \"follow-up\".")

L1 = r"""*Part C, follow-up (base change for the fundamental groupoid).* BISITE proves for small categories: every covering is an intrinsic fibration for the localizer `W_1` of `π_1`-equivalences (B.13), and a finite bifibration with fibre `BC_2` over the face poset `K` of the boundary of the tetrahedron is not (B.12): the `C_2`-central extension `H → K` given by the 2-cocycle supported on the chain `{0} ⊂ {0,1} ⊂ {0,1,2}` has `π_1(H) = 1` and `π_1(H ×_K H) = C_2`. A lead, unverified: a functor `p: E → B` whose strict fibres are its homotopy fibres lies in `Fib_{W_1}` if and only if `π_2(B, b) → π_1(F_b, x)` vanishes for all `b`, `x`. (1) Prove or refute the lead, and its analogue for `W_0` (trivial action of `π_1(B)` on `π_0` of the fibres). (2) State and prove the theorem for topoi in his generality: for a 2-cartesian square of topoi as at scan 166, the square of multi-Galois topoi is 2-cartesian if and only if an obstruction you define vanishes; identify the obstruction with a class in `H²` of the base with coefficients in the band of the fibre, and with the gerbe of §20. (3) Compute it in his cases: `B_{X,U} → X` for a curve `X` of genus 0 and of genus `g ≥ 1`, with one and with several missing points; decide from it his rejected statement at scan 129 and say exactly which coefficients (torsion; locally constant; of order prime to what) make `H^*(B_{X,U}, F) → H^*(X_ét, f^*F)` an isomorphism. (4) Prove his van Kampen theorem as a cosheaf statement: for an open cover the multi-Galois topos is the 2-colimit; then decide the closed version: for a closed subtopos and its open complement, prove that the 2-pushout needs the "topos at the missing points" as third term, by exhibiting the failure without it (the example `(0,1] → [0,1]` restricted to `0`, BISITE D.4, transported to topoi) and proving the corrected square. (5) Write a program that, for finite categories `E → B` given by tables, computes `Π_1(E ×_B X)` and `Π_1 E ×_{Π_1 B} Π_1 X` for all `X → B` with at most four objects, and run it on `H → K` and on every bifibration over the five-element monoid `{1, e_00, e_01, e_10, e_11}` (`e_ij e_kl = e_il`) with fibre a group of order at most 4. Where a statement resists, prove it for `B` a 1-type and for `B = S²` and give the smallest undecided square."""

L1D = r"""*Part D, follow-up.* The neutrality question of (b) and the obstruction of Part C, follow-up (2), are the same kind of class: prove that the gerbe of scans 240–241 is the obstruction to base change of the fundamental groupoid for the bundle he considers, compute it, and compare with the non-neutral `C_2`-gerbe over the 2-sphere of BISITE B.12."""

L2 = r"""*Part E, follow-up (the multi-Galois topos as a fine theory).* BISITE proves that for the localizer `W_0` of bijections on `π_0` the coarse relative theory `(Cat/S)[W_{0,S}^{-1}]` identifies the universal covering `EC_2 → BC_2` with the identity of `BC_2`, while the fine theory (maps tested on the comma categories `s\X`) is `Fun(S^op, Set)` and keeps them apart (E.6, B.16). (1) Prove that for every small category `S` the multi-Galois topos of `S` in his sense is the full subcategory of locally constant objects of the fine theory, for both variances, and that the two variances are identified on it by inversion in `Π_1 S`. (2) Prove the same for a locally connected topos, with his pro-variant of §3 in the non-locally-simply-connected case, and decide whether the pro-object or the prodiscrete localic groupoid is what the fine theory sees (give a topos where they differ). (3) Describe the kernel of the comparison from the fine to the coarse theory: prove that two locally constant objects become isomorphic in the coarse theory if and only if their total spaces have the same `π_0` over each component of `S`, or give the correct invariant. (4) Do (1) and (3) with `Set` replaced by groupoids (`W_1`), by `n`-types, and by `D(Z/ℓ)` (GALOIS Part C), as far as each goes: for each, which torsors or gerbes does the coarse theory lose."""

L2A = r"""*Part A, follow-up.* His §§1–3 take the category of locally constant objects as primary. Prove in this part, for a small category and for a locally connected topos, that it is the locally constant part of the fine relative theory for sets and not of the coarse one (statement and tasks in Part E, follow-up): with the coarse theory a torsor and the trivial torsor become isomorphic (BISITE E.6)."""

L3 = r"""*Part B, follow-up (fixed points, homotopy fixed points, classes of sections).* State and prove, or refute with the exact exception: for a finite group `Γ` acting on a compact surface `X` with negative Euler characteristic, possibly with boundary or punctures and possibly reversing orientation, the map from `X^Γ` to the space of sections of `X_{hΓ} → BΓ` is a weak homotopy equivalence. In his terms: (i) `π_0(X^Γ) →` {classes of sections of the extension} is bijective; (ii) the centralizer in `π` of a section subgroup is trivial when the fixed component is a point or an arc and infinite cyclic when it is a circle, so `π^Γ = 1` fails exactly in cases you list; (iii) the set of classes is finite, with a formula in terms of the signature of the action. Derive (i) from Nielsen realization and the geometry of the hyperbolic plane (Kerckhoff; the fixed points of a finite group of isometries of a tree or of `H²`) and say which step is the one he lacks; then give the purely group-theoretic proof for `Γ` cyclic of prime order and attack the dévissage he proposes at scans 292–293, proving it for `Γ` solvable and isolating what a simple `Γ` needs. Do the torus and the cylinder separately (his "seul contre-exemple", scan 179). Programs: for every Fuchsian signature with `Γ` of order at most 12 and genus at most 3, enumerate the extensions `E`, the conjugacy classes of finite subgroups of `E` mapping isomorphically to `Γ`, and compare with the fixed-point data of the signature; attach code and tables (this may share code with (e) above). BISITE gives the general frame: over `BΓ` the relative theory is that of groupoids over `BΓ` (E.10); a strict fixed-point category computes the homotopy fixed points only for fibrant diagrams (A.24, A.28); with coefficients in which `|Γ|` is not invertible the fixed-point functor is not a finite colimit (G.7, G.8)."""

L3C = r"""*Part C, follow-up.* With the statement of Part B, follow-up, and BISITE E.10, A.24, A.28: say what "Le plus agréable serait que ce soit un homotopisme" (scan 281) requires (which diagram must be fibrant; which fixed-point functor is meant), and prove that statement or the strongest true one."""

L6 = r"""*Part B, follow-up (filling a hole as a proper functor).* BISITE F.15–F.17 (reviewed only in its first reduction: treat it as a lead) proves for genus 0 that forgetting a marked point is a proper functor of entrance-path categories of the `M̄_{0,n}` (every outgoing lifting category is weakly contractible) and is not smooth: for `n = 4` one incoming lifting category is a `K(Z, 1)`, the kernel of the map `Z² → Z` of twist groups (LM3 F.11 proves the profinite form of this failure, with the cyclotomic action on it). (1) Check that proof and prove the statement for every `(g, n)` with `2g − 2 + n > 0`, using the curve complex and Kent–Leininger–Schleimer for the fibre; compute every non-contractible lifting category (that they are all of type `K(Z^r, 1)`, from Dehn twists that become equal after forgetting, is a guess from one computed case: prove or refute it). (2) Translate into his language: express his hole-filling functor on groups with loops and its sections ("forage") through this functor, and prove from properness the exact sequences he uses in §§15–18 and 23–25. (3) Do the same for a finite group `Γ` acting: prove or refute that the functor of `Γ`-fixed strata is proper, and compute the lifting categories at the strata with non-trivial stabilizer. (4) Decide whether the profinite completion of these categories keeps properness (the lifting categories are then profinite groupoids): prove it in genus 0 for `n ≤ 5`, where the groups are explicit."""

L4 = r"""*Part B, follow-up (strict against homotopy sections).* A model in which "section up to conjugacy" is a strict notion. For a functor `f: A → C` of small categories, PATHS E.5 and BISITE B.19 construct `E(f) → C`, a functor whose strict fibres are the homotopy fibres of `Bf`, natural in `f` (the path factorization). (1) Prove or refute: the category `Sec(f)` of functors `s: C → E(f)` with `p_f s = 1` and natural transformations over `C` has the weak homotopy type of the space of sections of the fibration associated with `Bf`. If it is false in general, prove it for `C = BG`, `G` a group, after replacing `BG` by the category of a free resolution you specify, and prove that `π_0` is then the set of `π_1(F)`-conjugacy classes of splittings when `A → BG` is the functor of a group extension. (The reviewer checked one case by hand: for `e → BZ` there is no strict section.) (2) Use it to write his injectivity statement ("Je dis que cette application est injective") as: for an anabelian curve `X` over `K`, the map from `X(K)` to `π_0` of the pro-finite analogue of `Sec` is injective; prove it by his argument and by the Kummer argument, and say which of his axioms ① to ⑥ is used where. (3) For his pathological lifts (scan 413), decide which of the six axioms each violates in this model. (4) Program: for `G = C_2`, `C_3`, `S_3` acting on a finite connected graph (as a category), compute the classes of strict sections of `E(f)` restricted to paths of length at most `N` and compare with the conjugacy classes of splittings of `π_1 ⋊ G`; find the least `N` that gives the right count in each case."""

L4C = r"""*Part C, follow-up.* The statement of LM4_FINITE_GROUP_ACTIONS, Part B, follow-up (fixed points against homotopy fixed points for a finite group on a surface) contains (b) for `Γ = Z/2` generated by an anti-involution: if that answer is attached, derive (b) from it; if not, prove here that `X^σ → X^{hσ}` is a weak equivalence, with the components that are circles giving sections with infinite cyclic centralizer."""

